# Claim, lock & fence

If the document is the store and many agents write to it, you need a database's
discipline: don't let two writers stomp the same row. The work format does this with
**drafts** and **scopes** — no global mutex.

A **draft** is a scoped, uncommitted overlay; it commits atomically or not at all. A
**scope** narrows what the draft may touch — here, one task — so two agents claiming
two different tasks never contend. That narrowed scope is the **fence**.

server :claims do
  # compare-and-swap: open a draft fenced to one task, commit only if still unclaimed.
  def claim(title, who) do
    draft scope: {:task, title} do
      case fetch(title) do
        {:ok, %Task{owner: nil} = t} -> commit(%Task{t | owner: who, status: :doing}); {:ok, :claimed}
        {:ok, %Task{owner: other}}   -> {:error, {:already_claimed, other}}
        :error                        -> {:error, :no_such_task}
      end
    end
  end

  # only the owner who claimed it may close it — the fence stops one agent closing another's work.
  def finish(title, who) do
    draft scope: {:task, title} do
      case fetch(title) do
        {:ok, %Task{owner: ^who} = t} -> commit(%Task{t | status: :done}); {:ok, :done}
        {:ok, %Task{owner: other}}    -> {:error, {:not_owner, other}}
        :error                         -> {:error, :no_such_task}
      end
    end
  end
end

Because the draft commits over `:tasks` — the live `data` from [[Plan]] — the board
updates the moment a claim lands. The page isn't a snapshot of the store; the page
*is* the store.
